Got it, that makes total sense now — the code is working correctly, you just haven't set up the actual Gmail credentials yet, so it fails right at the "send OTP" step. That's expected, not a bug.

To fix it, you need to:

Have a real Gmail account you're okay using for sending emails (create one if needed, e.g. yourprojectname@gmail.com)
Turn on 2-Step Verification on that Google account: https://myaccount.google.com/security
Once that's on, generate an App Password here: https://myaccount.google.com/apppasswords
Select "Mail" as the app (or "Other" → name it "InterviewApi")
Google gives you a 16-character password like abcd efgh ijkl mnop
In your .env, add:
   EMAIL_SENDER=yourprojectname@gmail.com
   EMAIL_PASSWORD=abcdefghijklmnop

(no spaces, no quotes)
5. Restart your server fully (stop it, not just let nodemon reload)

Now, the other thing you noticed is a real design issue worth fixing — you're right to flag it:

Right now, createStudentIdentity saves the student to the database first, then tries to send the OTP email. If the email fails, the student record already exists — so when you retry registering with the same email, you correctly get "email already exists," but that user can never get their OTP and is stuck unverified forever.

Once your email credentials work, you'll want to fix this properly. A common pattern:

js
// after saving createUserData successfully...
try {
  await SendOtpToMail(email, otp);
} catch (err) {
  // rollback: delete the just-created record so registration can be retried
  await Student.findByIdAndDelete(savePendingData._id);
  return res.status(500).json({
    message: 'Registration failed while sending OTP. Please try again.'
  });
}

Or alternatively, in resendOTP, allow it to work even without checking isVerified differently — but the rollback approach above is cleaner since it doesn't leave orphaned unverified accounts cluttering your database.

